---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { helpers } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const h = helpers();
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Sun by Month: Who Publishes These Holiday Weather Tables';
const description = 'Who publishes Sun by Month, why its holiday weather averages come from named NOAA stations and sea data, what it never does, and how to report a wrong figure.';
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Sun by Month</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Search for where is hot in a given month and you mostly find travel agents and tour operators. Their tables rarely say where a figure comes from, which years it covers, or whether "sea temperature" means an average, a peak or a guess. Some pages update their dates every year while the numbers stay the same. We wanted the opposite: one table per month and per place, built from public measurements, with the station named and the number of years shown, so that you can check any figure yourself.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds tools on public data. It is an independent site, not a travel agent or a tour operator. It sells no holiday, takes no commission, carries no affiliate link and asks for no contact details.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What it relies on</h2>
    <p>Two public datasets from NOAA, the US government agency for weather and oceans: its archive of daily weather station records (GHCN-Daily) and its analysis of sea surface temperature (OISST), for {h.total} places and the period 1991 to 2020. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists every station and every rule; the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains how corrections are made.</p>
    <h2>What it leaves out</h2>
    <p>Forecasts, sunshine hours (for lack of an official source we could read), prices, and any advice on health or safety abroad, for which the Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office publishes travel advice.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, for a station that looks wrong or a place you would like added.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
